course: dictionary
lesson: 2041
title: Congratulations and good news
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can tell you how someone feels even when you do not know every letter of it. «Congratulations!» is what people say to show they are happy for you. A boy in Ontario sees a pop-up that says «Congratulations!» on his screen, so he knows someone is pleased with his news.
explain: The clues are in the situation. When a baby arrives, the kind reply is «congratulations», so the word must mean that you share the family's joy. When a judge smiles and says «Congratulations», she is praising the person in front of her. Ask yourself: who is speaking, and what has just happened?
explain: Sometimes the words mean the opposite. If someone says, «Well, congratulations, you broke it», they are not praising you. The tone and the situation show they are cross or sarcastic. Read the clues around the word, and try your meaning in the sentence to check that it still makes sense.
fact: Congratulations is a word you say to show you are happy for someone who has done well or had good news.
fact: «Congratulations!» is an exclamation, said to someone on their success or on a happy event such as a new baby or a wedding.
fact: Congratulations is almost always used in the plural form. The singular congratulation is formal, as in «a letter of congratulation».
fact: Useful phrases are «Congratulations on …» and «Congratulations to …», such as «Congratulations on passing your test».
fact: «Congrats» is a short, informal way of saying congratulations, often used with friends.
exercise: The judge smiles, signs the papers and says «Congratulations.» Why does she say it? | *To praise her and share her happiness | To ask the woman to leave the court | To tell her she is in trouble | To say the court is closed
exercise: When a baby arrives in a family, what is a kind reply? | Be quiet about it | *Congratulations | Nothing, because babies are boring | Go away and leave them alone
exercise: Which phrase would you use to say you are happy that your friend passed a test? | Go away, I am cross | *Well done, congratulations on passing your test! | Not bad, I suppose | Congratulations on passing!
test: What does the formal word «a letter of congratulation» mean? | *The act of congratulating someone | A birthday card for a dog | A letter about a lost teddy | A letter asking a question
test: Ben's mum says, «Well, congratulations, you broke it.» What does she really mean? | She is very proud of him | She is giving him a prize | She wants him to break it again | *She is cross, so the word is sarcastic
exam: Which form is almost always used for the word of praise, as in «Congratulations!»? | Congratulate | *Congratulations | Congratulating | Congratulation
